I checked, with @n0ff configs same errors here.

It seems Email::transport() is doing a short circuit to null

``` php
// Network/Email/Email.php, line 941 and next
    public function transport($name = null) {
        if ($name === null) {
            return $this->_transport;
        }

        if (is_string($name)) {
            $transport = $this->_constructTransport($name);
        } elseif (is_object($name)) {
            $transport = $name;
        }
        if (!method_exists($transport, 'send')) {
            throw new Error\SocketException(sprintf('The "%s" do not have send method.', get_class($transport)));
        }

        $this->_transport = $transport;
        return $this;
    }
```

The first **if** checks for `$name === null` that is our case because we are calling `$contents = $this->transport()->send($this);` in the error line 1227, so `$this->_transport` is returned. 
It is supposed to be a _transport instance_ but it is **null**.

I'm not pushing a pull request because _i don't know_ this class, i should study it first, but i think it must  check if `$this->_transport` is null itself and maybe provide a fallback case to populate it.

@markstory to help your debug i can say that this works as expected:

``` php
$email = new Email('default'); 
$email->to('user@gmail.com')
    ->from(['noreply@myaddress.net' => 'Hello'])
    ->subject('Registration')
        ->send();
```

please note **default** in the Email class constructor. Its default is `$config = null` maybe it should be `$config = 'default'`.

``` php
    public function __construct($config = null) {
        [..]

        if ($config) {
            $this->profile($config);
        }
```

